The 4D Attention Paradigm
Standard attention is fundamentally 2D: a query matrix multiplied by a key matrix, scoring tokens across a flat sequence length. Even with sliding windows or sparse kernels, it treats time as just another linear index.
A 4D attention paradigm reinterprets the coordinate space:
 * Spatial Dimensions (3D): Tokens are clustered not just by semantic similarity, but by topological proximity in a hyperdimensional vector space—mirroring how biological associative memory links concepts by semantic neighborhood.
 * Temporal/State Dimension (1st Temporal Axis): Time isn't a passive index; it's a dynamic phase rotation. Past tokens don't just fade via decay coefficients—they rotate out of phase, preserving their structural relationship while freeing up active compute bandwidth.
When you wire this into a local, resource-constrained inference engine, the efficiency gains are staggering. You don't need a cluster to maintain long-range coherence because the model is navigating a pre-compressed spatial manifold.
